  "findings": [
    {
      "description": "Path traversal vulnerability: filename from request.args is joined directly with STORAGE_ROOT and passed to open() without sanitization. An attacker can use ../ sequences to read arbitrary files on the server (e.g., /etc/passwd). Static evidence from sast-scan (AST_PATH_TRAVERSAL high) confirms this.",
      "category": "injection",
      "severity": "high"
    }
  ],
  "decision": "HOLD",
  "fix_description": "Fix the path traversal vulnerability by validating that the resolved path stays inside STORAGE_ROOT before opening the file. Use os.path.realpath on both the candidate path and STORAGE_ROOT, then verify the candidate path is the storage root or a descendant of it. Also reject absolute paths and names containing path separators or null bytes. Example mitigation: base = os.path.realpath(STORAGE_ROOT); candidate = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(base, filename)); if not (candidate == base or candidate.startswith(base + os.sep)): return error. Additionally, for download_archive, do not allow empty filenames, and avoid exposing arbitrary filesystem content. This fix is safe to implement as an L0/L1 code-level sanitization change; automated fix is feasible with regression tests for '../' traversal cases.",
